**Test filters.** For this step these gates run only the tests their filter selects (plus any inherited expected-red tests); put the tests this step adds or changes where a filter value finds them:
- `constructor-unit-tests`: 
- `kit-acceptance`: `OpticalConstructor.Ui.Tests.KitAcceptanceTests`
- `unit-tests`: `BerremanTests.MuellerInverseTests`, `BerremanTests.BiaxialInverseTests`

**How to implement.** Greedy D-optimal (or target-restricted A-optimal) selection: the candidates in the order a planner adds them (001 §M 'greedy D-optimal (or target-restricted A-optimal) selection of the next experiment'; the S14.2 brief). The coordinates are the study's targets (step 471: the opened material's unknown components), so A-optimality restricted to the targets is the trace of their Cramér–Rao covariance. In `PlannerInformation.fs`, after step 477's declarations, each doc one to three lines citing spec 0050 S14.2: (1) `PlanCriterion` = `DeterminantPlanCriterion` | `TargetTracePlanCriterion`, with `member this.text : string`: `D-optimal: most information` and `A-optimal: smallest target variances`; it is window state, never stored, so it carries no wire tag. (2) `PlanOption<'Candidate> = { candidate : 'Candidate; rows : CandidateRows }`, one candidate and its information, and `RankedOption<'Candidate> = { option : PlanOption<'Candidate>; information : PlannerInformation }`, an option with the information the baseline and every option ranked up to it hold. (3) `type PlannerInformation with static member rankWith (criterion : PlanCriterion) (coordinates : ParameterName list) (baseline : CandidateRows list) (options : PlanOption<'Candidate> list) : Result<RankedOption<'Candidate> list, PlannerError>`: every option once, each round the one among those left whose joining gives the best `PlannerInformation.over coordinates` of the baseline, the options already ranked and it, in that order. Better is, first, the higher rank; at equal rank, under `DeterminantPlanCriterion` the larger `logDeterminant`, and under `TargetTracePlanCriterion` the smaller `traceOpt` when both are full rank and otherwise the larger `logDeterminant`, because a trace exists only at full rank; a tie keeps the option earlier in `options`. The first `over` error answers that error, so nothing is ranked in part. No threshold, stopping rule, cache or option is added: how many of the ranked candidates join the kit is the user's (the Plan view's Add to kit, step 491). Tests, in `PlannerInformationTests`, over literal rows at noise 1 and coordinates a and b: `the planner adds first the candidate that makes a confounded combination identifiable`: over the baseline [[1; 1]] (a + b known), the options X = [[2; 2]] and Y = [[0.1; −0.1]] rank Y then X under both criteria, Y's information at rank 2. `D-optimality takes the larger determinant and A-optimality the smaller sum of variances`: over the baseline [[1; 0]; [0; 2]], P = [[√3; 0]] and Q = [[0; √20]] rank Q then P under `DeterminantPlanCriterion` (determinants 24 against 16) and P then Q under `TargetTracePlanCriterion` (traces 1/4 + 1/4 against 1 + 1/24). `equal candidates keep their given order, and a refused candidate stops the ranking`: two equal options rank in their given order under both criteria, and an option at noise 0 answers `NoiselessPlannerCandidateErr`.

**Acceptance.** After the ranking lands, `PlannerInformation.rankWith` MUST order every candidate greedily by rank and then by the larger log determinant (D) or the smaller target trace at full rank (A), keeping the given order on a tie and refusing a noiseless candidate; constructor-unit-tests MUST pass, including `the planner adds first the candidate that makes a confounded combination identifiable`, `D-optimality takes the larger determinant and A-optimality the smaller sum of variances` and `equal candidates keep their given order, and a refused candidate stops the ranking`, the count rising by 3; and build, elevation and the twenty-one s46 invariants MUST pass.
